// Jacobi iteration on a 2D grid — CAP-HIT (did-NOT-converge) variant.
//
// Landed TASK-0453.05 (rigour epic P5). This is the SIBLING of
// 21-jacobi-converge that deliberately exercises the OTHER runtime
// branch of the `for..until` machinery: the cap-hit / did-NOT-converge
// path. Where 21-jacobi-converge breaks EARLY (the convergence
// predicate fires at generation 30 < cap 64), THIS example sets the
// compile-time cap BELOW the convergence generation so the predicate
// NEVER fires within the cap — the loop runs the full
// `0 .. ITERS_CAP+1` worst case and the runtime extracts the last
// computed generation, emitting the cap-hit stderr diagnostic.
//
// WHY this example exists
// -----------------------
// The bounded `for..until` surface is the static-firing-order-preserving
// form of data-dependent iteration: the Petri/soundness gate analyses
// the FULL-N unroll (the worst case), and any early-exit prefix `0..k`
// is a sub-trace of that bounded net (bounded a fortiori). The
// soundness argument therefore RESTS on the worst-case (full-cap)
// replay actually being a real, value-correct execution path. Example
// 21-jacobi-converge converges at k=30 < cap, so it only ever runs the
// CONVERGED branch end-to-end; the cap-hit branch (sentinel stays at
// -1, `__nuc_final_gen` resolves to the cap, the did-NOT-converge
// stderr diagnostic) was covered ONLY by string-level unit tests
// (forward-carried gap from TASK-0341.02.01.06 review, P3-2). This
// example closes that gap: it RUNS the full-cap worst case end-to-end
// and is byte-identical vs an independent reference oracle, so the
// "worst-case replay is bounded by the cap" claim the thesis makes for
// bounded data-dependent iteration is a tested reality, not an
// assertion.
//
//   field[t][y][x] = (field[t-1][y-1][x] + field[t-1][y+1][x]
//                   + field[t-1][y][x-1] + field[t-1][y][x+1]) / 4
//                   (t >= 1, y in 1..H-1, x in 1..W-1)
//   field[0][y][x] = seed[y][x]   (t == 0, seed via the same kernel)
//
//   maxdiff[t]     = max over the interior of
//                    |field[t][y][x] - field[t-1][y][x]|   (L-infinity)
//
//   for t : 0 .. ITERS_CAP+1 until maxdiff[t] <= TOL
//
// What this example demonstrates (vs 21-jacobi-converge):
//   - The CAP-HIT runtime branch: with the SAME seed and SAME TOL=2 as
//     21-jacobi-converge (whose `maxdiff[t]` first drops to <= 2 at
//     generation 30), but ITERS_CAP=16 (< 30), the `until` predicate
//     never fires inside the cap. The loop runs all 17 generations
//     (t = 0..ITERS_CAP), `__nuc_break_gen` stays at its -1 sentinel,
//     and `__nuc_final_gen` resolves to ITERS_CAP — the last computed
//     generation (TASK-0341.02.01.05.03). The output is `field[16]`,
//     value-correct vs the reference oracle.
//   - The cap genuinely BOUNDS the loop at runtime (the worst-case
//     full replay the soundness gate analyses is a real execution).
//   - The cap-hit stderr diagnostic (`[[nuc_converge]] did NOT
//     converge ...`) is observable — NOT a silent stop. It is
//     stderr-only, so the cross-backend differential's `output.bin`
//     bytes are untouched (determinism-safe).
//
// What this example does NOT demonstrate:
//   - Multi-worker / DISTRIBUTED `for..until`. As 21-jacobi-converge:
//     the single-worker `naive` schedule runs byte-identically on ALL 7
//     tier-1 backends (every backend delegates a host-only schedule to
//     the shared single-worker emitter — epic S7, TASK-0341.02.01.08).
//     A DISTRIBUTED (collective-break) convergence schedule stays out of
//     reach (16-jacobi/distributed blockage + collective all-reduce +
//     broadcast machinery — the remaining scope of epic S7); the
//     multi-worker walkers correctly stay fail-loud on `break_cond` for
//     a partitioned schedule until that lands.
//   - Floating-point arithmetic. As 16-jacobi / 21-jacobi-converge,
//     integer `/ 4` truncating division is used for bit-determinism
//     across schedules and backends.
//
// WHY field[ITERS_CAP] is LOAD-BEARING here (the cap-hit value bites)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// On cap-hit, `__nuc_final_gen` == ITERS_CAP, which is exactly the
// source-level extraction index `field[ITERS_CAP]`, so the runtime
// final-read REWRITE (TASK-0341.02.01.05.02) is a NO-OP for this
// example — that rewrite is exercised by 21-jacobi-converge's EARLY
// exit instead. What this example's differential bites on is the
// CONTROL: with ITERS_CAP=16 the interior at generation 16 is still
// mid-diffusion (NON-zero, and DISTINCT from the seed `field[0]` and
// from any earlier generation), so a compiler that
//   (a) broke early when it should not (predicate sense inverted /
//       off-by-one), or
//   (b) failed to run the full cap (read an unwritten later slice),
// would extract a DIFFERENT generation and diverge from reference.bin.
// The break gen 30 (with this seed + TOL=2) is strictly GREATER than
// the cap 16, so the predicate provably never fires inside the cap and
// the cap-hit branch IS the path taken.
//
// Single-assignment shape (PRD §6.2.1) — IDENTICAL to 21-jacobi-converge
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Five top-level `data` symbols, each the LHS of exactly one Dataflow
// statement. `partials` and `maxdiff` are indexed by the generation `t`
// so the per-generation reduction does not multi-assign a single slot.
//   - `seed`     — loaded once via `load_input()`.
//   - `field`    — full per-generation state `i32[ITERS_CAP+1][H][W]`.
//   - `partials` — per-generation L-infinity accumulator `i32[ITERS_CAP+1]`.
//   - `maxdiff`  — per-generation convergence scalar `i32[ITERS_CAP+1]`.
//   - `result`   — final (last-computed) generation extracted via `ident`.

const H         : usize = 8;        // grid height
const W         : usize = 8;        // grid width
const ITERS_CAP : usize = 16;       // compile-time cap, BELOW the break gen 30 -> cap-hit
const TOL       : i32   = 2;        // L-infinity convergence tolerance (same as 21)


// Whole-program input, full per-generation state, per-generation
// reduction scalars, and the last-computed-generation output. Each is
// single-assignment at the symbol level.
data seed     : i32[H][W];
data field    : i32[ITERS_CAP+1][H][W];
data partials : i32[ITERS_CAP+1];
data maxdiff  : i32[ITERS_CAP+1];
data result   : i32[H][W];


// Pure 5-tap Jacobi step with a seed-fallback path for t == 0. Same
// kernel + same six-arg signature as 16-jacobi / 21-jacobi-converge.
kernel jacobi5_or_seed : (i32, i32, i32, i32, i32, i32) -> i32 pure;

// 3-arg pure L-infinity accumulator (the 07-matmul `madd` shape):
// `max_abs_acc(acc, new, old)` returns `max(acc, |new - old|)`. The
// `|new - old|` is OVERFLOW-SAFE (i64-widening; TASK-0436).
kernel max_abs_acc : (i32, i32, i32) -> i32 pure;

// Identity copy. Stages the per-generation accumulator into the
// `maxdiff[t]` scalar AND stages the last-computed `field[ITERS_CAP]`
// cells into the flat `result` array.
kernel ident : (i32) -> i32 pure;

// I/O kernels. Effectful: ordering preserved, never duplicated.
kernel load_input  : ()         -> i32[H][W] effectful;
kernel save_output : (i32[H][W]) -> ()        effectful;


// Dataflow.
//
// 1. Load the seed.
seed <-- load_input();

// 2. Iterate UNTIL converged or the cap. Identical body to
//    21-jacobi-converge; only ITERS_CAP differs (16 here, so the
//    `until maxdiff[t] <= TOL` clause never halts inside the cap and
//    the loop runs the full worst case).
//
//    maxdiff[0] = max |field[0] - field[ITERS_CAP]| = max |seed - 0|
//    (the zero-init prev slice), which is non-zero, so the loop never
//    halts at t==0.
for t : 0 .. ITERS_CAP+1 until maxdiff[t] <= TOL {
    // (a) Compute generation t.
    for y : 1 .. H-1 {
    for x : 1 .. W-1 {
        field[t][y][x] <-- jacobi5_or_seed(
            field[(t + ITERS_CAP) % (ITERS_CAP + 1)][y-1][x],
            field[(t + ITERS_CAP) % (ITERS_CAP + 1)][y+1][x],
            field[(t + ITERS_CAP) % (ITERS_CAP + 1)][y][x-1],
            field[(t + ITERS_CAP) % (ITERS_CAP + 1)][y][x+1],
            seed[y][x],
            t
        );
    }}
    // (b) Per-generation L-infinity reduction over the interior.
    for ay : 1 .. H-1 {
    for ax : 1 .. W-1 {
        partials[t] <-- max_abs_acc(
            partials[t],
            field[t][ay][ax],
            field[(t + ITERS_CAP) % (ITERS_CAP + 1)][ay][ax]
        );
    }}
    // (c) Stage the accumulator into the convergence scalar the
    //     `until` clause reads.
    maxdiff[t] <-- ident(partials[t]);
}

// 3. Extract the last-computed generation into a flat 2D array. Source-
//    level this reads `field[ITERS_CAP]` (the cap slice); on cap-hit the
//    single-worker pthreads-sync backend resolves `__nuc_final_gen` to
//    ITERS_CAP (TASK-0341.02.01.05.03), so the rewrite is a no-op and
//    the cap slice IS the converged-as-far-as-possible generation.
for ry : 0 .. H {
for rx : 0 .. W {
    result[ry][rx] <-- ident(field[ITERS_CAP][ry][rx]);
}}

// 4. Save the last-computed generation.
save_output(result);
